      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        104,
        107
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " We are given a decimal string such as `12.34e56`. This string consists of integral (`12`),",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        108,
        202
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " fractional (`34`), and exponent (`56`) parts. All parts are optional and interpreted as a",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        203,
        296
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " default value (1 or 0) when missing.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        297,
        337
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        338,
        341
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " We seek the IEEE 754 floating point number that is closest to the exact value of the decimal",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        342,
        438
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " string. It is well-known that many decimal strings do not have terminating representations in",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        439,
        536
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " base two, so we round to 0.5 units in the last place (in other words, as well as possible).",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        537,
        632
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Ties, decimal values exactly half-way between two consecutive floats, are resolved with the",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        633,
        728
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " half-to-even strategy, also known as banker's rounding.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        729,
        788
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        789,
        792
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Needless to say, this is quite hard, both in terms of implementation complexity and in terms",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        793,
        889
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " of CPU cycles taken.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        890,
        914
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        915,
        918
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " # Implementation",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        919,
        939
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        940,
        943
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " First, we ignore signs. Or rather, we remove it at the very beginning of the conversion",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        944,
        1035
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " process and re-apply it at the very end. This is correct in all edge cases since IEEE",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1036,
        1125
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " floats are symmetric around zero, negating one simply flips the first bit.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1126,
        1204
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1205,
        1208
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Then we remove the decimal point by adjusting the exponent: Conceptually, `12.34e56` turns",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1209,
        1303
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " into `1234e54`, which we describe with a positive integer `f = 1234` and an integer `e = 54`.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1304,
        1401
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " The `(f, e)` representation is used by almost all code past the parsing stage.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1402,
        1484
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1485,
        1488
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " We then try a long chain of progressively more general and expensive special cases using",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1489,
        1581
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " machine-sized integers and small, fixed-sized floating point numbers (first `f32`/`f64`, then",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1582,
        1679
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " a type with 64 bit significand). The extended-precision algorithm",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1680,
        1749
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " uses the Eisel-Lemire algorithm, which uses a 128-bit (or 192-bit)",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1750,
        1820
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " representation that can accurately and quickly compute the vast majority",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1821,
        1897
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " of floats. When all these fail, we bite the bullet and resort to using",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1898,
        1972
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " a large-decimal representation, shifting the digits into range, calculating",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1973,
        2052
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " the upper significant bits and exactly round to the nearest representation.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2053,
        2132
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2133,
        2136
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Another aspect that needs attention is the ``RawFloat`` trait by which almost all functions",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2137,
        2232
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " are parametrized. One might think that it's enough to parse to `f64` and cast the result to",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2233,
        2328
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " `f32`. Unfortunately this is not the world we live in, and this has nothing to do with using",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2329,
        2425
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " base two or half-to-even rounding.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2426,
        2464
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2465,
        2468
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Consider for example two types `d2` and `d4` representing a decimal type with two decimal",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2469,
        2562
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " digits and four decimal digits each and take \"0.01499\" as input. Let's use half-up rounding.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2563,
        2659
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Going directly to two decimal digits gives `0.01`, but if we round to four digits first,",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2660,
        2752
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " we get `0.0150`, which is then rounded up to `0.02`. The same principle applies to other",
      "path": "doc",
